/**
 * @defgroup optimal_specific_speed_correction_calculator Pump Specific-Speed Efficiency Correction
 * @ingroup equipment_and_property_calculators
 * @brief Estimates the efficiency decrement associated with pump style and specific speed.
 * @details
 * How much should the pump-efficiency estimate be reduced for the supplied specific speed?
 * This empirical correction supplies a decrement to the separately calculated, viscosity-adjusted
 * efficiency. It does not represent the pump efficiency or a measured loss in a particular machine.
 *
 * @heading{Final Result and Interpretation}
 * The returned correction is a dimensionless efficiency fraction to subtract:
 * @formula{optimal-specific-speed-correction-efficiency; \eta_{avg}=\eta_{visc}-C_{Ns}}
 * @symtable
 * @symrow{\eta_{avg}; Corrected average pump-efficiency estimate as a fraction; \unitless}
 * @symrow{\eta_{visc}; Separately calculated viscosity-adjusted efficiency as a fraction; \unitless}
 * @symrow{C_{Ns}; Returned specific-speed efficiency decrement; \unitless}
 * @endsymtable
 *
 * A correction of about 0.01894 subtracts approximately 1.894 percentage points. It is not a
 * multiplier and does not mean an efficiency of 1.894%. The separate pre-correction calculator
 * returns percent, so convert that estimate to a fraction before applying viscosity and this
 * decrement. The maximum-efficiency estimate requires a separate deviation factor.
 *
 * @heading{Select the Pump-Style Family and Speed Interval}
 * Supply specific speed in the US customary convention rpm*sqrt(US gpm)/ft^(3/4). The
 * @ref optimal_specific_speed_calculator "Pump Specific Speed" calculation obtains that value
 * from rotational speed, supplied flow and head per stage. A dimensionless specific speed or
 * an SI-convention number cannot be substituted directly into these empirical curves.
 *
 * Only vertical-turbine style uses the vertical curves. Every other style uses the nonvertical
 * curves, including axial flow, specified-optimal-efficiency and positive-displacement styles.
 * Axial flow's shared pre-correction flow curves do not make its specific-speed correction
 * vertical. Neither a supplied-efficiency override nor a positive-displacement model is provided.
 *
 * | Family | Lower curve | Upper curve |
 * | --- | --- | --- |
 * | Vertical turbine | Specific speed &lt; 4550 | Specific speed &gt;= 4550 |
 * | All other styles | Specific speed &lt; 2530 | Specific speed &gt;= 2530 |
 *
 * Equality selects the upper curve. The method does not interpolate or smooth between curves;
 * these thresholds select an empirical expression rather than define validated operating ranges.
 *
 * @heading{Evaluate and Convert the Selected Polynomial}
 * Let x be the numerical specific-speed value in the stated US customary convention. The
 * selected polynomial gives a correction in percentage points. Lower-clamp it and convert to
 * an efficiency fraction:
 * @formula{optimal-specific-speed-correction-polynomial;
 *   P=a_0+a_1x+a_2x^2+a_3x^3+a_4x^4+a_5x^5+a_6x^6,\qquad
 *   C_{Ns}=\frac{\max(P,0)}{100}
 * }
 * @symtable
 * @symrow{x; Numerical value of specific speed divided by one unit of rpm*sqrt(US gpm)/ft^(3/4); \unitless}
 * @symrow{a_0,\ldots,a_6; Selected coefficients for the numerical specific-speed convention; \percent}
 * @symrow{P; Polynomial correction before the lower clamp; \percent}
 * @symrow{0; Lower correction bound; \percent}
 * @symrow{100; Percentage-point scale per unit fraction; \percent}
 * @symrow{C_{Ns}; Returned efficiency decrement as a fraction; \unitless}
 * @endsymtable
 *
 * The lower clamp prevents a negative polynomial from increasing the estimated efficiency.
 * There is no upper clamp: extrapolation can return a decrement greater than one. The powers
 * and coefficients describe an empirical fit; they do not identify separate physical losses.
 * Carry unrounded intermediate values through the weighted sum.
 *
 * | Curve | a0 | a1 | a2 | a3 | a4 | a5 | a6 |
 * | --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
 * | Nonvertical, lower | 14.884085 | -0.021342141 | 0.0000121172 | -0.0000000031885957 | 1.4907927E-13 | 1.2855352E-16 | -2.3798184E-20 |
 * | Nonvertical, upper | 6.5703317 | -0.010048327 | 0.0000061475098 | -0.0000000019659725 | 3.5079978E-13 | -3.2272033E-17 | 1.1881188E-21 |
 * | Vertical, lower | 14.788539 | -0.022490348 | 0.000017226359 | -0.0000000075690542 | 1.8893534E-12 | -2.4834106E-16 | 1.3345507E-20 |
 * | Vertical, upper | 4.0559384 | -0.0026632249 | 0.0000006761157 | -0.000000000087766773 | 6.5103425E-15 | -2.5380141E-19 | 4.007753E-24 |
 *
 * @heading{Worked Example: Slurry Pump at Specific Speed 1170}
 * Slurry is nonvertical and 1170 is below 2530, so use the lower nonvertical curve:
 * @formula{optimal-specific-speed-correction-example;
 *   P=14.884085-0.021342141(1170)+0.0000121172(1170)^2
 *     -0.0000000031885957(1170)^3+1.4907927\times10^{-13}(1170)^4
 *     +1.2855352\times10^{-16}(1170)^5-2.3798184\times10^{-20}(1170)^6
 *     \approx1.8943\%,\qquad C_{Ns}\approx0.01894
 * }
 * @symtable
 * @symrow{P; Example polynomial correction before the lower clamp; \percent}
 * @symrow{1170; Numerical US customary specific-speed value; \unitless}
 * @symrow{14.884085,\ldots,-2.3798184\times10^{-20}; Lower nonvertical polynomial coefficients; \percent}
 * @symrow{C_{Ns}; Example efficiency decrement as a fraction; \unitless}
 * @endsymtable
 *
 * The positive polynomial is retained. Starting from a separately supplied viscosity-adjusted
 * fraction of 0.84, subtracting the unrounded correction gives about 0.8211, or 82.11% average
 * efficiency. This example demonstrates subtraction of percentage points, not multiplication
 * by a loss percentage. The correction alone cannot establish achievable pump performance.
 *
 * @heading{Assumptions, Arithmetic Boundaries and Inappropriate Uses}
 * The curves do not establish a validated speed range or fit uncertainty. Evaluate applicability
 * with the pump duty and an appropriate performance curve. Do not use extreme extrapolation as
 * evidence of a physical efficiency loss, or treat a zero correction as proof of optimal operation.
 *
 * No input validation or defaults are applied. Zero specific speed gives about 0.14884 for the
 * nonvertical family and 0.14789 for vertical turbine. Negative inputs are evaluated directly;
 * they are not accepted operating duties. The arithmetic uses fmax with zero: a NaN polynomial
 * yields zero, so zero is not a validation-success flag. Positive infinity yields zero for both
 * families; negative infinity yields positive infinity for vertical turbine and zero otherwise.
 * Very large finite inputs can overflow polynomial terms and reach the same zero fallback.
 * These retained arithmetic outcomes are not engineering error codes or supported extrapolations.
 *
 * @see optimal_specific_speed_correction::calculate
 */
